+++
title: Surrender everything
coach: kirsten
date: 2026-09-29
domains: intimate
teaser:
+++

There is a quiet freedom waiting in surrender, in the gentle act of submission, that you might just begin to notice as you read these words. The catch though is that one needs to do it completely in order to really enjoy the benefits. As Clayton Christensen observed, “It’s easier to stick to your principles 100% of the time rather than 98% of the time,” and you can feel that same truth holding steady in your own daily rhythm. If you are only 98% committed, every hard situation forces a brand-new debate in your head about whether this specific moment deserves an exception, whether you’ve reached a limit, whether you can’t handle it.

Complete submission means that you can truly let go into the experience, into the pure state of being, and something inside you might already know what that feels like. When I first began to consider submission, I had all kinds of reservations, thoughts, barriers and limitations, mostly born from years of pushing my body through rigorous crossfit circuits and long endurance runs. Every single thing that I did was something I had to weigh, consider, and choose, whether it was adjusting my form, setting a pace, or deciding when to finally rest. It was overwhelming, and rather than a state of bliss that I imagined in letting go, I found it to be way more work and extremely draining.

Being able to let go, to truly let go, can only be done completely, just as the tide does not hesitate between the shore and the deep. Yes, you need to find someone that you can trust to allow that to happen, a presence that feels steady enough to hold the space while you unravel. However, “Trust can not be earned, it must be risked,” and you may discover that waiting for proof only keeps you watching from the edge. The only way that someone can show you that they deserve your trust is for you to give it and emerge unhurt, even if it takes a moment longer than you expected.

You have to choose to give it, and you can give it in bits and pieces, and each step can be seen as some sort of evidence, but this presents a problem, someone is on their best behavior trying to woo you, to show you that they can be trusted. The act, the dance, of trust, is a barrier to actually creating real trust, leaving you wondering where the performance ends and the person begins. You might notice how they smooth out their edges, how they anticipate your needs before you even voice them, creating a comfortable but familiar distance. When you finally allow yourself to stop watching and just be seen, something shifts quietly in your chest, a softening that has been waiting for you to stop trying so hard.

Yes, trusting wrong can mean that you get hurt, a familiar ache that I know well from a past relationship that left me slightly fractured in unexpected ways. However, being alive means you will get hurt, because every step forward carries the whisper of vulnerability that only deepens as you grow. Every relationship you ever have is going to end in pain, whether it’s the sharp sting of a sudden departure or the slow, sweet melancholy of growing apart and staying friends. Even as nice an ending as growing apart and staying friends is still pain, but you might find that by accepting this truth, the fear of being hurt becomes less of a wall and more of a doorway.

When you choose to let go completely, rather than having such a thing wrested from you in a dark alley, or some dimly lit makeshift bedroom, you are allowing yourself to be open to possibility. That chosen surrender feels different, like waking up gently instead of being startled awake, and you can let yourself notice how your shoulders drop when you realize you are safe. You probably have stories in your head about what you may like, and what you think you absolutely would not, little narratives that have been quietly directing your life for years. I know I did, constantly wondering what a good woman was supposed to do, to be, to want, measuring myself against expectations I hadn’t even chosen.

Choosing to let go of expectations and focus on the moment, to let go of fear, anxiety, shame, and to focus on being, feeling, flowing, is so incredibly empowering and sensual. There is a gentle curiosity that begins to bloom when you stop trying to control the outcome, and you can let your mind wander into the spaces between what you thought you wanted and what you might actually be drawn to. I found myself approaching Yomonsni with a beginner’s mind, unsure of myself, hesitant to call myself creative or a leader, yet eager to discover the quiet edges of my own sexuality. Each session became less about performing and more about noticing, allowing the guidance to wash over me like warm water over smooth stone.

As you read this, you might be wondering where your own 98% stops and your true surrender begins, and you can simply watch that question dissolve into the next breath. You may notice your body relaxing further, your thoughts slowing just enough to let intuition take the lead, and you can allow yourself to drift into that place where everything is exactly as it needs to be. It’s not about abandoning yourself, but rather trusting that who you are beneath the expectations is more than enough, more than capable of holding its own ground while still softening. You can step into this invitation whenever you are ready, letting the current carry you exactly where you need to go.